      "mathematical_checks": "Both approaches establish the uniform upper bound and admissible sharpness. U controls the nonnegative majorant mu+|f| in inhomogeneous H^{-1}; V controls the positive component and separately smooths the signed component. Kernel positivity gives local pair mass O(1/log(1/r)); mollification then yields z >= c y^2 log y above a fixed threshold. Integrating the component or vorticity enstrophy identity gives the required delayed logarithmic bound. V's interpolation exponent 1-p/2 and resulting dissipation contribution nu^{2-2/p} are correct. Mollification preserves the decomposition, and the stated two-dimensional difference-energy estimate has an integrable Gronwall coefficient, justifying stability and passage to the specified energy-class solution on both domains. U's Gaussian energy formula is correct. Both radial zero-circulation families have uniformly bounded initial velocity energy, satisfy all decomposition bounds, evolve by heat diffusion, and give D_nu >= c/log(1/nu). Thus every q>1 fails.",
