      "mathematical_checks": "The chain gives a complete proof for arbitrary real-valued functions. Every transition has ordered destinations L<R and current state (L+2R)/3, including both boundary exceptions. Odd N ensures the exceptional destination is on the grid. Repeated right moves strictly increase the state and give a uniform positive absorption probability for each fixed finite chain. Numerical states and function values are bounded for fixed N, so the limiting expectations are justified without an unrestricted optional-stopping argument. Positive-to-negative entry occurs only at -1/N, and negative states cannot return to positive ones. Starting at -1/N gives 2q_2+3q_3=1/N and hence negative absorption probability at most 1/(2N). The terminal distribution therefore converges to masses 2/3 at 0 and 1/3 at 1. Only four fixed function values enter this limit, so no continuity, measurability, or density assumption is used. Increasing affine rescaling proves the missing reversed-order inequality and rules out the proposed function.",
